<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanmenxia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanmenxia is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> near the gorge that gives it its name. In the Zhou period it was the site of Shangyang, capital of the State of Guo, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangcunling-cemetery">Shangcunling cemetery</a> has yielded rich burials. The Sanmenxia Dam, completed in 1960, was the first large dam built on the Yellow River.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanmenxia.jpg" alt="Sanmenxia" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 周长武 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smxgongcheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sanmenxia is the westernmost prefecture-level city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to the east, Nanyang to the southeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> to the north, and lies on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> at the point where the river cuts through the Loess Plateau on its way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. Mountains surround it on three sides. The name means the Gorge of Three Gateways and comes from two islands that split the river into three channels. According to Chinese mythology <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a> cut the ridge three times with a divine axe to prevent flooding, and the gates were named the Gateway of Man, the Gateway of Gods and the Gateway of Devils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Western Zhou period the area belonged to the State of Western Guo, ruled by relatives of the Ji family of Zhou. Guo moved its capital from the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a> to Shangyang, next to Sanmenxia, and the territory was later annexed by the State of Jin. Archaeological finds near the city between 1956 and 1991 revealed mass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> graves and bronzeware of the Western Guo rulers.</p><p>In the Western Han and Tang periods, when the capital lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the grain tribute passed upstream through the gorge. The rapids were dangerous, with losses of 20 to 50 per cent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, which was one reason for moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. Several projects to bypass the rapids with canals or by removing boulders failed, and from 150 CE gallery roads were built for towpaths. The Sanmenxia Dam, built in the late 1950s, began to flood the ancient passes, and archaeologists studied them intensely before they were submerged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city administers two districts, two county-level cities and two counties. The excavated Shihao Section of the Xiaohan Ancient Road, near Sanmenxia, forms part of the World Heritage Site Silk Roads: the Routes Network of Chang'an-Tianshan Corridor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanmenxia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today the Yangshao site in Mianchi County, <b>Sanmenxia</b>, which gave the culture its name, has a museum next to the excavations.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yangshao-culture">Yangshao culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the end of the Western Zhou, its rulers moved east to Shangyang, near modern <b>Sanmenxia</b> in Henan, controlling a key crossing on the Yellow River.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-guo">Western Guo</a></li></ul></section>
